\section{Applying Ohm's Law to Difficult Problems}

A harder circuit rarely needs a new formula. It needs the familiar laws used in a disciplined order. The central question is always: \emph{what p.d. is actually across this component, and what current is actually through it?}

\begin{mdframed}[backgroundcolor=green!5,leftline=true,linecolor=green!60!black,linewidth=2pt]
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textbf{Step \arabic*:},itemsep=4pt,topsep=3pt]
  \item \textbf{Redraw the operating circuit.} Replace a closed switch or ideal ammeter by wire; remove an open branch or ideal voltmeter.
  \item \textbf{Mark nodes and current directions.} Every uninterrupted ideal wire is at one potential. Choose a current direction if none is shown.
  \item \textbf{Use conservation before calculation.} Current entering a junction equals current leaving; potential rises equal drops around a closed loop.
  \item \textbf{Apply $V=IR$ locally.} Use the p.d. across, and current through, the particular resistor under consideration. Do not automatically use the battery voltage.
  \item \textbf{Solve and check.} Branch currents must recombine, loop voltages must balance, and a negative current only reverses the chosen direction.
\end{enumerate}
\end{mdframed}

\myskip
\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Question.} A $5.0$ V battery supplies a $1\,\Omega$ resistor in series with a junction. After the junction, a $2\,\Omega$ branch and a $3\,\Omega$ branch reconnect to the battery. Find all three currents without first using an equivalent-resistance formula.

\begin{center}
  \begin{circuitikz}[x=1cm,y=1cm,>=stealth,line width=1pt]
    \draw (0,0) to[battery1,l=$5.0$ V] (0,3);
    \draw (0,3) to[R,l=$1\,\Omega$,i>^=$I$] (2.5,3);
    \draw (2.5,3) to[R,l=$2\,\Omega$,i>^=$I_2$] (5.5,3);
    \draw (2.5,3) -- (2.5,1.3) to[R,l_=$3\,\Omega$,i>_=$I_3$] (5.5,1.3) -- (5.5,3);
    \draw (5.5,3) -- (5.5,0) -- (0,0);
    \node[circle,fill,inner sep=1.2pt] at (2.5,3) {};
    \node[circle,fill,inner sep=1.2pt] at (5.5,3) {};
  \end{circuitikz}
  \captionof{figure}{A $1\,\Omega$ resistor before two parallel branches. The branch p.d. is unknown, so call it $V_p$ rather than assigning the full 5.0 V to each branch.}
\end{center}

\textbf{Solution.} Let $V_p$ be the p.d. across the parallel section. Ohm's law on each branch gives
\[
I_2=\frac{V_p}{2},\qquad I_3=\frac{V_p}{3}.
\]
At the junction, current is conserved:
\[
I=I_2+I_3=\frac{V_p}{2}+\frac{V_p}{3}=\frac{5V_p}{6}.
\]
Around the whole loop, the battery's 5.0 V rise equals the drop $I(1\,\Omega)$ plus the branch drop $V_p$:
\[
5=I+V_p=\frac{5V_p}{6}+V_p=\frac{11V_p}{6}.
\]
Hence
\[
V_p=\frac{30}{11}\text{ V},\qquad
I=\boxed{\frac{25}{11}\text{ A}},\quad
I_2=\boxed{\frac{15}{11}\text{ A}},\quad
I_3=\boxed{\frac{10}{11}\text{ A}}.
\]
Check: $I_2+I_3=25/11$ A, and the loop drops are $25/11+30/11=5$ V. Notice also that the smaller-resistance branch carries the larger current.
\end{exercise}

\myskip
\begin{exercise}
\textbf{Question.} A metal wire is connected to a fixed $5.0$ V supply. Its resistance rises from $5\,\Omega$ at $25^\circ$C to $12\,\Omega$ at $200^\circ$C. The initial current is $1.0$ A. Find the hot-wire current and explain the change.

\textbf{Solution.} The supply keeps the p.d. fixed, not the current. At the higher temperature,
\[
I=\frac{V}{R}=\frac{5.0}{12}=\boxed{0.42\text{ A}}.
\]
Heating a metal increases its resistance, so the current falls at fixed p.d. This also shows why a filament lamp is not ohmic across a wide range: its temperature, and therefore its resistance, changes.
\end{exercise}

