content.text:
You are correct. The original wording treated two different statements as though one logically proved the other:

1. Ohm’s law applies when temperature and other physical conditions are held constant.
2. A filament lamp has a non-linear \(V\)-\(I\) curve during normal operation because its temperature is **not** held constant.

The filament-lamp observation therefore does not prove that tungsten disobeys Ohm’s law at constant temperature.

I corrected this distinction throughout [content.tex](/Users/sinchunyeung/Documents-local/Students/Emily/18-7-2026/notes/content.tex). It now explains that:

- constant temperature is a controlled condition of Ohm’s law;
- “ohmic” means the measured \(V\)-\(I\) relation is proportional over a stated range and under stated conditions;
- “non-ohmic” means the measured relation is non-linear under those operating conditions;
- a filament lamp is non-ohmic **in normal operation** because changing the voltage/current also changes its temperature;
- this does not imply that tungsten violates Ohm’s law when its temperature is fixed.

The corrected [main.pdf](/Users/sinchunyeung/Documents-local/Students/Emily/18-7-2026/notes/main.pdf) compiled successfully, and there are no linter errors.

