\textbf{Ohm's law.} For a conductor whose temperature and other physical conditions are kept constant, the current through it is directly proportional to the p.d. across it:
\[
\boxed{V\propto I\quad\text{and hence}\quad V=IR\text{ with constant }R.}
\]
The constant-temperature condition belongs to the law itself: it tells us what must be controlled before concluding that $V/I$ should remain constant. A component is described as \textbf{ohmic} over a stated range when its measured $V$--$I$ relation is a straight line through the origin under the stated conditions. It is described as \textbf{non-ohmic} when that measured relation is not proportional.

