  \node[blue,above,font=\footnotesize] at (5.7,3) {same potential};
\end{circuitikz}
\captionof{figure}{The uninterrupted red wires form one node, and the uninterrupted blue wires form the other node.}
\end{center}

For the top branch, use $V=IR$ for the two series resistors:
\[
\mathcal E=V_{2\Omega}+V_{4\Omega}
=2I_{\rm top}+4I_{\rm top}
=6I_{\rm top},
\qquad
I_{\rm top}=\frac{\mathcal E}{6}.
\]
For the bottom branch,
\[
\mathcal E=V_{6\Omega}+V_{6\Omega}
=6I_{\rm bottom}+6I_{\rm bottom}
=12I_{\rm bottom},
\qquad
I_{\rm bottom}=\frac{\mathcal E}{12}.
\]
The voltage at $A$ is the drop across the $4\,\Omega$ resistor measured from the blue node, while the voltage at $B$ is the drop across the right-hand $6\,\Omega$ resistor. Therefore, using $V=IR$ on those components,
\[
1=4I_{\rm top}-6I_{\rm bottom}
=\frac{2\mathcal E}{3}-\frac{\mathcal E}{2}
=\frac{\mathcal E}{6},
\qquad
\boxed{\mathcal E=6\text{ V}}.
\]

\textbf{Solution.} When the switch is closed, the coloured copy below shows that the two left-hand resistors connect between the same red and green nodes. They therefore have the same voltage across them.

\begin{center}
\begin{circuitikz}[x=1cm,y=1cm,>=stealth,line width=1pt]
  \draw (0,0) to[battery1,l_=$6$ V] (0,3.6);
  \draw[red,line width=1.6pt] (0,3.6) -- (1.2,3.6);
  \draw[red,line width=1.6pt] (1.2,3.6) -- (1.2,3);
  \draw (1.2,3) to[R,l=$2\,\Omega$] (4.2,3);
  \draw[red,line width=1.6pt] (1.2,3.6) -- (1.2,1.4);
  \draw (1.2,1.4) to[R,l_=$2\,\Omega$] (2.7,1.4)
        to[nos,l=switch] (4.2,1.4);
  \draw[green!60!black,line width=1.6pt] (4.2,3) -- (4.2,1.4) -- (4.2,1.0);
  \draw (4.2,1.0) to[R,l_=$2\,\Omega$] (4.2,-0.6);
  \draw[green!60!black,line width=1.6pt] (4.2,-0.6) -- (5.0,-0.6) node[ground,scale=0.8] {};
  \fill (4.2,1.0) circle (1.8pt) node[right=2pt] {$A$};
  \fill (4.2,-0.6) circle (1.8pt) node[right=2pt] {$B$};
  \node[red,above,font=\footnotesize] at (0.9,3.6) {same potential};
  \node[green!60!black,right,font=\footnotesize] at (4.2,1.8) {same potential};
\end{circuitikz}
\captionof{figure}{Colouring the duplicate circuit: the red wire is the positive node, and the green wire is the node containing A and the closed-switch junction.}
\end{center}

First calculate the current through the two identical left-hand resistors. Because they connect across the same two nodes, both have the same voltage $V_L$. The total current through them is the sum of their currents:
\[
I_L=\frac{V_L}{2}+\frac{V_L}{2}=V_L.
\]
Thus the two branches together draw the same current as a single $1\,\Omega$ section, but we have calculated this from $V=IR$ rather than introducing parallel notation.

This $1\,\Omega$ section is in series with the right-hand $2\,\Omega$ resistor, so the same total current $I$ flows through both sections. Applying $V=IR$ to the complete series path,
\[
6=I(1+2),
\qquad I=2\text{ A}.
\]
The right-hand resistor is connected between $A$ and the earthed point $B$. Applying $V=IR$ to that component,
\[
V_A-V_B=I(2\,\Omega)=(2\text{ A})(2\,\Omega)=4\text{ V}.
\]
Since $V_B=0$,
\[
\boxed{V_A=4\text{ V}}.
\]

